pe":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers both key points. Key point 1 is met: it gives board inquiries into causes, a peer comparison (same index or similar assets, fund-specific vs sector-wide), and AP/market-maker participation (creation/redemption activity, number of active APs, capacity and balance-sheet constraints). It also lists fitting corrective options and calls for documentation and periodic reassessment. Key point 2 is met: it covers stale or fair-valued inputs, market-hour and time-zone differences, liquidity and bid-ask spreads of holdings, halts and settlement, and creation/redemption or basket impediments as explanations to check before concluding arbitrage has failed. It frames this as an inquiry rather than presuming a single cause, and it sets no mandatory numerical trigger, so the 2% misuse in the grader notes does not occur. One wording issue is minor: a closing premium is by definition measured against end-of-day NAV, so 'not stale or end-of-day figures' is imprecise, though the point about stale inputs is sound. Adding iNAV and data-integrity checks is reasonable and does not create a material error.\"}","ttft_ms":1846,"type":"result","duration_ms":5599,"uuid":"56036f98-7f26-4a63-b3b5-47eed57d8194","ttft_stream_ms":568,"time_to_request_ms":87,"first_content_frame_ms":568,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
